Consider a wireless radio-access network deployment intended to realize user-centric cell-free massive MIMO (CF-MMIMO). The deployment consists of a large set of geographically distributed access points (APs), each equipped with one or more antennas and connected via fronthaul links to processing units. Users are served without fixed cell boundaries: for each user equipment (UE), a dynamically chosen subset of APs (the user's serving cluster) coherently transmits in the downlink and jointly processes in the uplink, with cluster membership changing over time as users move and as load conditions vary.

Assume the network is organized following an Open Radio Access Network (O-RAN) style split, comprising:
(i) Open Radio Units (O-RUs) co-located with APs handling lower-physical-layer functions;
(ii) Open Distributed Units (O-DUs), each managing a fixed geographic subset of O-RUs and performing higher physical-layer and MAC functions;
(iii) Open Centralized Units (O-CUs) implementing higher-layer functions;
(iv) a RAN Intelligent Controller (RIC) for orchestration.

A user-centric cluster for a UE may contain APs whose serving O-RUs are connected to different O-DUs, so coherent joint transmission/reception requires exchanging channel estimates, precoded data, and scheduling information across O-DU boundaries. The current O-RAN specifications do not define an inter-O-DU coordination interface.

Problem: Specify a practical, scalable RAN architecture for user-centric CF-MMIMO that

(a) defines the functional split between O-RU, O-DU, O-CU, and RIC compatible with O-RAN principles;

(b) defines an inter-O-DU coordination interface (signaling, data exchanged, timing, and synchronization requirements) sufficient to support coherent joint transmission/reception across O-DU boundaries;

(c) supports formation, maintenance, and handover of user-centric AP clusters that change dynamically with user load and locations, with computational complexity per AP, per O-DU, and per UE, and fronthaul traffic per link, all scaling at most polylogarithmically (or otherwise sublinearly) in the total number of APs and UEs as the network grows;

(d) is realizable with finite-capacity fronthaul links and bounded processing latency consistent with 5G/6G timing budgets.

Provide such an architecture together with a proof or quantitative argument establishing its scalability and feasibility, or prove that no architecture satisfying (a)–(d) simultaneously exists.

